gm-method (two reusable lessons). (1) WORKFLOW with gm-method subagents: to fan out a breadth audit where every subagent works the gm way, prepend each agent() prompt with a GM preamble ('hypothesize -> VERIFY by reading the REAL source file:line or running it, never guess; the witness IS the finding; read-only, the structured finding is your return value') rather than relying on a registered 'gm' agentType - portable, and the pipeline(pairs, audit->adversarial-verify(real&&worthIt)->synthesize) shape returns confirmed file:line findings each of which becomes a fix row. A 36-agent audit reliably surfaces the real call-sites + their CAVEATS (e.g. a shared client/server fn whose contract a one-sided change would break) that a solo pass misses. (2) WINDOW-vs-SELF worker blocker: an SDK that exposes a capability via `if (typeof window !== 'undefined') window.__x = ...` is UNREACHABLE in a Web Worker (worker has `self`/`globalThis`, no `window`) - so a worker-side consumer silently falls back. FIX at the SDK source: expose on `globalThis` (covers both window and worker self). Witness the blocker by adding a one-line diagnostic at each early-return in the consumer's init (warn the exact reason) then reading the worker console - 'X never appeared after init' pinpointed the window-only exposure vs the GL/import/init succeeding. General: when a browser-proven capability fails in a headless/worker context, suspect `window`-gated globals first.